WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Forms are where interfaces
ask people for effort.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.380
Every unclear label or silent
error costs them time,

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.940
and for screen reader users an
unlabelled field can be a dead end.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.060
You will replace placeholders
with real labels,

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:21.020
connect hints and errors to the input, and put
a summary at the top when several fields fail.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.900
The placeholder vanishes as
soon as someone starts typing.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:27.160
It usually has low contrast.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.000
Many screen readers do not
treat it as a reliable name.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.320
A visible label stays put.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.160
Clicking it focuses the input, and
assistive technology announces it.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:43.340
The autocomplete attribute lets browsers
and password managers fill the field,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.320
which helps everyone, and especially
people with motor or memory impairments.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:52.280
Format requirements belong in
a hint connected to the input,

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:58.200
not hidden in a tooltip. aria-describedby
makes the hint part of what is announced

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.280
when the field receives focus.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.270
A good error says what went
wrong and how to fix it,

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:07.500
appears next to the field,
and is connected to it.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.400
aria-invalid tells assistive
technology the field has a problem,

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:15.700
and aria-describedby reads the message.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.100
Write the message as an instruction.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:25.180
“Enter an email address like name@example.com”
is more useful than “Invalid input”.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.080
When a form with several
errors is submitted,

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:33.380
move focus to a summary at the top that
lists each error as a link to its field.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:37.640
People using a screen reader hear that
something went wrong straight away,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.220
and everyone gets a quick
way to jump to each problem.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.820
Related controls, such as
a set of radio buttons,

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.100
belong in a fieldset with a legend.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.440
The legend is announced with
each option, so “Delivery speed,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.780
Standard” makes sense on its own.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.140
Every input needs a visible,
programmatically connected label.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:04.720
Use aria-describedby for hints and
errors, and aria-invalid when a field

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.920
fails. Write errors as instructions, and
give multi-error forms a focused summary.
